{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>From the wilderness of Kedemot.</b> From the place that was a wilderness before the Holy One, blessed be He, brought forth water for them from the rock — therefore it is called 'wilderness of Kedemot,' because beforehand it was a wilderness and now it is a place of flowing waters. And since most of the land of Sihon had belonged to Ammon and Moab, they offered them peace, but to Og, who held the land of Rephaim, they did not offer peace, for one does not offer peace to the seven nations. Another interpretation: 'From the wilderness of Kedemot' — from the eternity of the world I learned that he should have killed the Egyptians immediately, and even so Moses and Aaron sent to call to Pharaoh for peace, and said to him, 'Let my people go and serve me.' And also at the time of the giving of the Torah, it was revealed before the Holy One, blessed be He, that the children of Esau and the children of Ishmael did not want to accept the Torah, and even so the Holy One, blessed be He, called them to accept it, as it is said: ״וזרח משעיר למו, הופיע מהר פארן״. Therefore: 'And I sent messengers, etc.' — words of peace, saying. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Two letters he sent — one of peace from Moses, and one of war from Israel. Resh Lakish said: Heaven forbid that Israel disagreed with Moses; rather, in one letter they wrote peace above and war below — this is what the verse says: ״אני שלום, וכי אדבר, המה למלחמה״. And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: By your lives, you shall inherit their land in peace, as it is said: ״וענוים ירשו ארץ, והתענגו על רוב שלום״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:27.283038073Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}